      "id": "1e42fc9635a63add",
      "item_path": "config_store::read_config",
      "line": 21,
      "message": "`read_config` calls `try_deserialize_unchecked`, which does not verify the account discriminator. Any account owned by this program can then be read as this type, regardless of what it actually is.",
      "references": [
        "https://www.anchor-lang.com/docs/account-types",
        "https://github.com/coral-xyz/sealevel-attacks"
      ],
      "remediation": "Use `try_deserialize`, which verifies the discriminator, or the typed `Account<'info, T>` which does it before the handler runs. The `_unchecked` variants are for accounts you have just created and know the layout of.",
      "rule": "WT010",
      "severity": "high",
      "snippet": "    pub fn read_config(ctx: Context<ReadConfig>) -> Result<()> {"
    },
    {
      "column": 12,
      "confidence": "medium",
      "file": "WT008_unsafe_close/closer.rs",
      "id": "8af310d24a646694",
      "item_path": "closer::close_position",
      "line": 23,
      "message": "`close_position` sets an account's lamports to zero without clearing its data. The runtime only reclaims the account at the end of the transaction, so a caller can send lamports back within the same transaction and keep the account, its data intact.",
      "references": [
        "https://solana.com/developers/courses/program-security/closing-accounts",
        "https://www.anchor-lang.com/docs/account-constraints"
      ],
      "remediation": "Prefer Anchor's `close = destination` constraint, which drains the lamports, zeroes the data, and assigns the account to the system program. Closing by hand requires all three — zeroing the balance alone leaves the account revivable within the same transaction.",
      "rule": "WT008",
      "severity": "medium",
      "snippet": "    pub fn close_position(ctx: Context<ClosePosition>) -> Result<()> {"
    },
